  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 409 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nCurrently through tonight...\n\nOutside of the brutal heat and humidity, overall quiet\nconditions remain in place across the forecast area this \nafternoon. Looking aloft, upper air and satellite data show \nlittle change in the ridging dominating the pattern, centered \nroughly over the OK/TX/CO/NM border area...keeping flow across \nthe Central Plains west-northwesterly. At the surface, an \noverall weak frontal boundary has sunk south through the \narea...currently stalled out from west- central KS northeastward\ninto southeast NE. This boundary hasn't done much for \ntemperatures...with 3PM obs in the mid 90s to near 100, but \nseparating the more easterly winds to its north to the more \nsoutherly winds to its south. Not surprisingly, moisture has \npooled along/north of this boundary...with nasty dewpoints in \nthe upper 70s to mid 80s across most of our Nebraska counties, \nwhile a little more mixing is keeping dewpoints in our south in \nthe mid 60s-near 70. As a result, several automated sensors have\nbeen showing heat index values more in the 115-120 degree range\nat times. Obviously no changes made to the Extreme Heat Warning\nthat remains in place CWA-wide through 10PM this evening.\n\nOff to the west-northwest over the High Plains, been seeing the \ndevelopment of scattered thunderstorms this afternoon. Overall \nsubtle forcing aloft, along with upslope low-level flow to the \nnorth across portions of SD and the frontal boundary across \nwestern KS is helping with activity. The brutal heating is \nallowing for steeper lapse rates and no shortage of instability \n(SPC Meso page showing 3000-5000 j/kg of MLCAPE across the \nregion)...but deeper layer is shear is pretty low. Winds aloft \nare not strong, so not seeing quick movement with any of this \nactivity...more just gradually drifting east with time. \n\nThis evening on through tonight...there remains the potential\nfor showers/storms to continue making their way east through \nthe forecast area. Precipitation chances remain pretty broad in \nnature...models still showing some variation on just how much \ncoverage there ultimately ends up being. Not out of the question\nthat a few of these storms could be on the strong to marginally\nsevere side given the lingering instability...wind gusts near \n60 MPH look to be the primary hazard, and the entire CWA remains\nin the Day 1 Marginal Risk area.\n\nMid-Week...\n\nFor the Tuesday through Thursday time frame, overall no notable \nchanges in the forecast. Models show little change in the upper \nlevel pattern, with the main upper level ridge axis continuing \nto run roughly through the High Plains. There remain periodic \nchances for showers and thunderstorms...mainly during the mid-\nlate evening and overnight hours...as models continue to show \nthe potential for shortwave disturbances riding around the edge \nof the main ridging and sliding through the area. Precipitation \nchances Tue evening-night and again Wed evening-night remain \ngenerally in the 20-50 percent range...with models then showing \nthe potential for a stronger shortwave disturbance to dig \nfurther south, with more widespread 40-60 percent chances Thu- \nThu night. Still plenty of details to iron out with the \ntiming/coverage of those chances. \n\nMore noteworthy for the mid-week period is models showing a\nbreak from the heat from the past few days. Highs Tue-Thu are \nforecast in the upper 80s-mid 90s...with heat index values more \nin the 90s-near 100...vs the well above 100 from the past few \ndays.\n\nFriday and on...\n\nThe forecast for Friday on into the weekend dries out...but will\nsee if that ultimately ends up panning out that way. The upper\nlevel disturbance that moves through Thu-Thu night looks to \ngive the main ridge axis a better push back to the \nwest...leaving our area with more amplified north-northwesterly \nflow. At this point models aren't showing much in the way of \ndisturbances impacting the area. This shift in the ridging helps\nkeeps temps closer to normal for this time of year...generally \nforecast to range from the mid 80s to near 90 through this \nperiod.",
